# Knowl v0.7.0

This release adds gradual reconciliation when configured sources are removed
or replaced, preserves content rejection diagnostics, and fixes Azure SSH Git
wiki synchronization.

## Changes

- Configured source owners move through `active`, `retiring`, and `deleted`.
  Removing a source schedules bounded, durable page reconciliation: pages
  supported only by that owner are removed, while mixed-source pages are
  recomputed from their remaining active evidence. Immutable raw history stays
  available. Retirement completes only after an authoritative check finds no
  remaining page references.
- Replacing a filesystem source with Git creates an independent source owner,
  not a citation alias. Accept the replacement before removing the old source;
  reconciliation is gradual and does not promise an atomic global cutover.
- Rejected content plans retain page paths, rule codes, and dependency reasons,
  including when no safe changes remain (`plan.empty_safe_subset`). Invalid
  content is still not published.
- Git synchronization supports Azure SSH wiki repositories and resolves fetched
  commits correctly.
- The repository wiki now includes updated local Codex guidance and nested
  navigation catalogs. Existing accepted revisions and update history remain
  preserved.

## Installation

After publication, run from a local project root:

```bash
npx --yes @baldaworks/knowl@0.7.0 setup codex
```

The plugin, MCP launcher, and five native npm packages use version `0.7.0`.
Existing local Codex, HTTP service, and embedded Go workflows remain supported.

## Upgrade and recovery

Back up the complete workspace and durable store, and stop existing writers
before upgrading. Source retirement adds durable lifecycle and reconciliation
state; use the matching binary for that state. Prefer a forward fix over an
in-place downgrade once retirement begins: older code does not enforce retiring
source publication authority.

See [source retirement and recovery](../operations.md#gradual-source-retirement-status-and-recovery)
for status and retry commands, and [publishing](../publishing.md) for release
verification and distribution details.
